89 1113 #uuid "d134dbd9-abad-5169-a7f6-3f9e65bfe7ad" 13194139535813 true] [17592186367789 1145 "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(4{e}^{2t})^{′} \\\\ (0)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}+1\\cdot 0 \\\\ 0\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}+3\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(2{e}^{3t})^{′} \\\\ (2{e}^{3t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 6{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}+1\\cdot 2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}+3\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 6{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 6{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{2t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{2t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {e}^{2t}+1\\cdot {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\cdot {e}^{2t}+3\\cdot {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{2t} \\\\ 3{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,b}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,b) & =|\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2t} & 2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4{e}^{2t})\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}-(2{e}^{3t})\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =8{e}^{5t}-0 \\\\  & =8{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $b$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R,$ ${a,b}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system." 13194139535813 true] [17592186367790 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367791 13194139535813 true] [17592186367790 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367792 13194139535813 true] [17592186367790 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535813 true] [17592186367790 1041 "field-1" 13194139535813 true] [17592186367790 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367792 13194139535813 true] [17592186367790 1110 #uuid "1ed1ccef-3dbf-58dd-86fa-d3cb6fe9c269" 13194139535813 true] [17592186367791 1034 "isn't" 13194139535813 true] [17592186367791 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535813 true] [17592186367791 1111 #uuid "e2513fe3-9e91-506d-a04c-81b03761b53e" 13194139535813 true] [17592186367792 103